When a process instance is cancelled immediately after initiation (before any tasks are created), `BpmProcessInstanceStatusEventListener` implementations do not receive the cancellation event.

## Root Cause

`BpmTaskServiceImpl.moveTaskToEnd()` returns early when the task list is empty, preventing `runtimeService.deleteProcessInstance()` from executing. Without this call, the process completion event chain never fires.

```java
// Before: Early return prevents event
public void moveTaskToEnd(String processInstanceId, String reason) {
    List<Task> taskList = getRunningTaskListByProcessInstanceId(processInstanceId, null, null);
    if (CollUtil.isEmpty(taskList)) {
        return; // ← Blocks deleteProcessInstance() call
    }
    // ... task cancellation logic ...
    runtimeService.deleteProcessInstance(processInstanceId, reason);
}
```

## Changes

- Removed early return on empty task list in `moveTaskToEnd()`
- Wrapped task-specific logic in conditional block that only executes when tasks exist
- Process deletion now always executes, ensuring completion event triggers regardless of task state

```java
// After: Process deletion always executes
public void moveTaskToEnd(String processInstanceId, String reason) {
    List<Task> taskList = getRunningTaskListByProcessInstanceId(processInstanceId, null, null);

    if (CollUtil.isNotEmpty(taskList)) {
        // Cancel and terminate tasks
        // ...
    }

    // Always executes - triggers event via processProcessInstanceCompleted()
    List<Execution> executions = runtimeService.createExecutionQuery()
        .processInstanceId(processInstanceId).list();
    if (CollUtil.isNotEmpty(executions)) {
        runtimeService.deleteProcessInstance(processInstanceId, reason);
    }
}
```

## Impact

Preserves existing behavior when tasks exist. Enables event listeners to monitor cancellations during the gap between process initiation and first task creation.

